<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pliny the Elder</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">23/24–79 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Gaius Plinius Secundus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pliny the Elder was a Roman officer and writer whose Natural History, in 37 books, gathered knowledge of geography, peoples, animals, plants and minerals. Its descriptions are an important source for little-known towns and peoples across the empire, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He died while commanding the fleet at Misenum during the eruption of Vesuvius in 79 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder.jpg" alt="Pliny the Elder" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geoffrey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_Illustrazione_del_Lombardo_Veneto_Vol_3_Plinio_Secondo_300dpi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vast estates were common; <b>Pliny the Elder</b> reports that six landowners held half the province until Nero had them put to death and their land passed to the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Livy and <b>Pliny the Elder</b> held that the Raeti of the Alps were Etruscans driven into the mountains by the Gauls, and modern linguists group Etruscan with Raetic and Lemnian in a Tyrsenian family unrelated to any other.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pliny the Elder</b> counted 179 towns in the province besides many smaller communities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Incense was in heavy demand for temple ritual and funerals across the Near East and later the Greek and Roman worlds, and the profits of the trade made the Sabaeans proverbially rich in the eyes of classical writers such as Strabo and <b>Pliny the Elder</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pliny the Elder</b> credited Miletus with founding some ninety colonies, a figure that is probably exaggerated but reflects a real and sustained movement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Colossus fell in an earthquake, its remains lay where they had fallen for centuries; <b>Pliny the Elder</b> wrote that few people could wrap their arms around its thumb.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Novum Comum. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stabiae">Stabiae</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.7,14.5&amp;year=59" aria-label="Stabiae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4905 710 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Pliny the Elder"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5355 980h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5355 980h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.7,14.5&amp;year=59">What was here in 59 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…this alone is certain, that there is nothing certain, and that there is nothing more proud or more wretched than man.&#8221;</p><footer>Natural History, book 2 &#183; tr. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, 1855 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57493/pg57493-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nature has, in reality, bestowed no greater blessing on man than the shortness of life.&#8221;</p><footer>Natural History, book 7 &#183; tr. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, 1855 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60230/pg60230-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But with man,—by Hercules! most of his misfortunes are occasioned by man.&#8221;</p><footer>Natural History, book 7 &#183; tr. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, 1855 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60230/pg60230-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To assist man is to be a God; this is the path to eternal glory.&#8221;</p><footer>Natural History, book 2 &#183; tr. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, 1855 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57493/pg57493-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For all over the world, in all places, and at all times, Fortune is the only god whom every one invokes; she alone is spoken of, she alone is accused and is supposed to be guilty…&#8221;</p><footer>Natural History, book 2 &#183; tr. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, 1855 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57493/pg57493-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
